'''''Ctenosuchus''''' ({{lit|comb crocodile}}) is an [[extinct]] genus of [[thalattosuchia]]n crocodylomorph in the family [[Teleosauridae]], known from the [[Middle Jurassic]] ([[Bathonian]] age) [[Anoual Formation]] of Morocco. The genus contains a [[Monotypic taxon|single species]], '''''Ctenosuchus tenuidens''''', known from two specimens, both of which preserve partial skulls and [[osteoderm]]s.<ref name="Ctenosuchus" />

It is characterized by a unique arrangement of widely spaced, needle-like teeth unlike those of all other known [[crocodylomorph]]s. Other animals with a comparable dental morphology include [[mesosaur]]s (a group of [[Stem group|stem]] reptiles), [[ctenochasmatid]] pterosaurs, and some fish (e.g., [[Atlantic needlefish]], [[goblin shark]], [[longnose gar]], and some [[angler fish]]es). The interlocking teeth of ''Ctenosuchus'' may have been an adaptation for trapping prey, such as small [[crustacean]]s, fish, and soft-bodied animals.<ref name="Ctenosuchus" />

